// The rules of Smash's rooms (schema `room_rules`): the rooms tagged with the
// `smash` mode. `SmashRulesPlugin` declares them; tools that fold them outside
// a match read `smash_combat_rules()`. An edit here is played at the next
// start, with no Rust build.
(
    // Damage here is percent, so a jab deals 3 and only a hit the size of a
    // smash attack (12% or more) lands heavy.
    heavy_hit_damage: Some(12),
    combat: Some((
        // How far a launched body may steer its launch, in radians.
        di_max_angle: 0.31,
        // A launch grows by this fraction of the move's base launch per point
        // of the victim's percent, for a volume that states no growth of its
        // own. 38 of 40 authored knockback volumes state a median of 0.0200.
        knockback_growth: 0.02,
        // The robot's down air can rebound its attacker. Ambition uses that;
        // a platform fighter must not, because offstage it would save the
        // attacker instead of killing the victim.
        downward_hit: Spike,
        // Meteor lock: about 18 frames in which a spiked body cannot recover.
        // Long enough to kill offstage, short enough to survive over the
        // stage. The end of the window is the meteor cancel.
        meteor_lock_time: 0.30,
        // Rage, capped at 1.4x. Percent already makes a hurt fighter easier to
        // launch; the cap keeps a comeback a chance, not a coin flip.
        rage_per_damage: 0.004,
        rage_max_scale: 1.4,
        // Staling, floored at 0.55: nine landings of one move and it is worth
        // about half. Varying moves lets it recover.
        stale_step: 0.05,
        stale_floor: 0.55,
        // Staling reduces damage in full but launch only a little. At high
        // percent the launch is mostly the percent term, so full staling would
        // stop a fighter's best kill move from killing. At 0.30 a fully stale
        // move keeps 1 - 0.30 * (1 - 0.55) = 86.5% of its reach and deals 55%
        // of its damage.
        stale_knockback_influence: Some(0.30),
        // How steep the victim-percent curve is, as a multiplier on the
        // percent term alone. 1.0 is the original law. 1.25 is the smallest
        // swept value that ends the stock (guards in
        // `smash_in_the_host::ring_out`):
        //
        //     scale | 700% stale jab | lateral | 0% fresh
        //     1.00  | ALIVE          | 246.4px | poke
        //     1.25  | KO             | 523.4px | poke
        //     1.50  | KO             | 627.7px | poke
        //     1.75+ | KO             | 722px+  | poke
        //
        // 1.50 is a ceiling the table cannot show: two CPU floors fail at 1.50
        // and pass at 1.25
        // (`smash_cpus_damage_each_other::two_cpus_in_the_shipped_composition_damage_each_other`
        // and `the_repertoire_gets_used::every_authored_route_gets_pressed`).
        // A steeper curve separates the CPUs, so they exchange less. Run both
        // floors again before you make this larger.
        //
        // Measurement traps: this jab has `launch_dir: None`, so it kills
        // through the ceiling, not a side; measure the resolved launch, not
        // the trajectory maximum; pin the attacker's rage (an unpinned CPU at
        // 62% multiplies every cell by 1.248). A launch past the tumble
        // threshold is not a ring-out, so calibrate on the stage's knockout
        // verdict.
        //
        // 2026-10-18: back to 1.0. With `base_knockback_scale` below, the 700%
        // stale jab converts unaided, so the multiplier is unearned (the
        // ring_out guards in `smash_in_the_host` say so and pick the value).
        //
        // 2026-10-18, later: 1.25. A launch now slows at Ultimate's rate
        // (`launch_decay` in `match_rules.ron`) where it used to be held for
        // all of hitstun, and fighters fall slower. Re-swept:
        //
        //     scale | 700% stale jab | lateral
        //     1.00  | ALIVE          | 246.6px
        //     1.25  | KO             | 453.5px
        //     1.50+ | KO             | 519px+
        //
        // It multiplies only the percent term, so a 0% hit is unchanged.
        victim_percent_knockback_scale: Some(1.25),
        // Kill moves are authored explicitly, not inferred. Base knockback is
        // not role: a growth curve on base knockback overrode authored
        // `knockback_growth`, distorted deliberate high-base, low-growth
        // shoves, and did not reach throws. Movesets author the separation
        // instead (pirate f-smash growth/base 0.0256, up-smash 0.0413, jab
        // 0.020), derived from measured stage thresholds
        // (G_new = G_old * p0/p1 for strikes, * (p0+d)/(p1+d) for throws).
        growth_base: None,
        // How hard a hit throws a fresh body: the growing launch's base, times
        // this. Without it a tilt at 0% slid the victim 2-4 px and left the
        // attacker 12 frames behind ON HIT, and an up tilt lifted a body 1 px:
        // kills were calibrated through growth alone, so everything below kill
        // percent was a nudge. Measured over the 23-fighter roster
        // (`ko_envelope feel`, with the Sakurai angle, tumble at 300 and the
        // percent scale at 1.0): 2.5 puts the forward smash's median KO at
        // 100% and the down smash's at 120% (Ultimate: 88-104, 124-152), and a
        // tilt at 0% is even to 3 frames behind on hit (was 11), an up tilt
        // lifting its victim 11 px (was 1).
        base_knockback_scale: Some(2.5),
        // The Sakurai angle: a near-horizontal launch slides a grounded body
        // flat under a weak hit, is lifted to the full angle by a strong one,
        // and always leaves an airborne body at the full angle (Ultimate's
        // `361`: flat to KB 60, 38 degrees from KB 88, tumble above KB 80).
        // The speeds are those ratios of a tumble line of 300 (the stage's is
        // now 250, a little below the flat band's top). With it, a forward
        // smash at 60% rises 38 px where it rose 16.
        sakurai_angle: Some((flat_below: 225.0, full_at: 330.0, angle_deg: 38.0)),
        // A hit area drawn by a character's art is at least this much of its
        // owner's height across, each way. Sheets draw their areas pixel-true
        // round the limb, so a jab or a tilt was a sliver (down to 0.03 of a
        // body height squared, where the authored volumes are near 0.35). Only
        // the slivers grow.
        art_hit_area_min: Some(0.5),
        // Crouch cancel, 0.85x: a defensive read at low percent that does not
        // save anyone from a kill move.
        crouch_cancel_scale: 0.85,
        // No blanket mercy window, as in Smash. Repeat protection belongs to
        // the move: one hitbox cannot hit a body twice, and separate Active
        // windows can. A 0.2 s window would also stop George Booul's
        // `bivalence` launcher (0.42 s) from landing after its pop (0.30 s).
        hit_repeat_window_scale: 0.0,
        // Grab hold grows with percent (Ultimate's 90 + 1.7p frames: 1.5 s at
        // 0%, about 4.3 s at 100%). Percent is read at the grab, so pummels do
        // not extend the hold.
        grab_hold_base_seconds: 1.5,
        grab_hold_per_damage: 0.028333334,
        // Every hold ends, however hurt the captive is.
        grab_hold_max_seconds: 6.0,
        // 14.4 frames per press (Ultimate's rate), so mashing is a real
        // option.
        grab_mash_seconds: 0.24,
        // Teams decide who may hit whom. Global friendly fire would also make
        // teammates hittable.
        friendly_fire: false,
        // Clanking is off: a tuning decision, the mechanism is tested. At 9
        // damage (the genre's threshold) CPUs traded so often that nobody was
        // launched (`every_live_fighter_stays_inside_the_frame`) and
        // `the_cpu_charges_a_smash_and_techs_a_landing_in_some_match` failed,
        // even ground-only. Try 9.0 first after a play session.
        clank_damage_window: 0.0,
        // A clank pushes both fighters back: less than a launch, but out of
        // each other's next swing.
        clank_rebound_speed: 190.0,
        // Edge cancel: an aerial landed on a platform edge that slides off
        // cancels its landing lag.
        edge_cancel_recovery: Some(true),
        // B-reverse: a special pressed backward turns the fighter around.
        special_turn: Some(true),
        // Wavebounce: the turn also reverses drift. One rule with two
        // settings, so neither becomes a per-fighter hack. Both are feel
        // calls.
        special_turn_reverses_drift: Some(true),
        // Sudden death at 150%: a timed match that ends level goes to a point
        // where almost any clean hit kills.
        sudden_death_damage: Some(150),
        // One hit in six barks. A rate, not a cooldown: a cooldown makes the
        // first hit of every exchange bark, which players hear as a rhythm.
        // A starting value; tune by play.
        bark_chance: Some(0.16666667),
        // Losing the ledge to a trump pushes the previous holder off, so a
        // trump is a real edge-guard option. 260 px/s is a shove, not a kill.
        // A starting value; tune by play.
        ledge_trump_pop: Some(260.0),
        // Ultimate holds a trumped fighter for about 30 frames. The number is
        // a tuning value: its only source is a forum measurement seen through
        // a search summary (smash-parity-inventory.md, "Ledge occupancy").
        ledge_trump_lockout: Some(0.5),
        // Ultimate's rule: a recovering fighter can steal the edge back.
        // `Hog` is the other generation's rule.
        ledge_occupancy: Some(Trump),
        // Double-jump cancel: an aerial from an air jump ends the jump's rise.
        // A feel call.
        double_jump_cancel: Some(true),
    )),
)
